  </div>

  <p>
    If you are still unsure which route genuinely fits your branch, timeline, and family
    situation, a session inside
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>
    can help you run this comparison with an actual person instead of guessing alone.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Whichever route you shortlist, treat the exam as one part of a bigger plan, not the
      whole plan.</strong> The engineers who come out ahead, whether the exam lands or not, are the
      ones who also build a real skill portfolio during the prep months: technical depth for their
      branch, visible proof of work (projects, a GATE-adjacent certification, a documented design or
      coding sample), the ability to explain that work clearly in an interview, and an honest read of
      their own financial runway. That combination is what actually opens high-income opportunities
      and moves you toward earlier financial freedom, with or without a rank card.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="timelines">Realistic prep timelines, not motivational-post timelines</h2>

  <p>
    Online forums love a "cleared it in 3 months" success story. Most real timelines look far less
    dramatic.
