  <h2 id="overlap">Can you prepare for more than one at once?</h2>

  <p>
    To a real extent, yes, and most successful aspirants do this deliberately rather than by
    accident.
  </p>

  <p>
    GATE and ESE share substantial technical-syllabus overlap in your branch's core subjects, so
    many aspirants build that foundation once in year one, then split effort toward ESE's General
    Studies and Engineering Aptitude paper or toward GATE's PSU and M.Tech application windows as
    each exam's cycle approaches. SSC JE and RRB JE share overlapping General Engineering and
    reasoning content, but sit at a shallower technical depth than GATE or ESE, so treat them as a
    separate, faster-cycle lane rather than a natural extension of GATE prep.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      A workable pattern for many engineers: pick one primary, higher-ceiling route (GATE-PSU or
      ESE) as the main bet, and keep one faster, lower-cost application open in parallel (SSC JE
      or RRB JE), so a full year is never spent with nothing to show for it if the primary route's
      cycle does not work out.
    </p>
  </div>

