  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Do not try to hold six routes in your head and decide this week.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Shortlist one primary route from this page using the 4-Checkpoint Protocol above, based on
      your actual branch eligibility, family runway, and appetite for a multi-year exam grind.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep one faster, lower-cost route open in parallel so your preparation year is never a
      complete blank if your primary attempt does not land the first time.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    A government job is one way to reach income stability, not the only skill decision that
    matters. Whichever route you pick, keep building real proof of your engineering skill
    alongside the exam prep, because the right skill portfolio is what protects you if a specific
    exam cycle does not go your way, and it is what actually moves you toward earlier financial
    freedom over a full career, not just the first offer letter. Move through that comparison with
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>
    if you want a second opinion, or start with the free
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a>
    if you are still unsure which route fits.
  </p>

